  "details": "The hub, as it is, has gotten overcrowded.  It has become more or less and undesirable place to be.  Many people that enter the hub only do so on their way to somewhere else. This change would hopefully alleviate some of that pressure and recentralize the hub as the core of VRC.\n \n\nWhat is a “SubHub”?\nCurrently the way things are set up, players spawn directly into the hub.  This change would add an intermediary space to which a player would load before directly entering the rest of VRC.  Users would be assigned a specific instance to which they would always load.  They would share this instance with as many, or as few, of their friends as they like.   \n\nCommunal SubHub\n\tThese hubs would form around a friend group or community.  While many default options would exist, groups would have the option to customize this world to fit their own tastes.   In this method, they could feature any number of worlds pertinent to that group.  Ideally the main hub would be featured as one of these worlds to encourage its use and help better tie the community as a whole together.  The main hub would then become the place users would go to find out more about the broader community as a whole.\n\n\nSolo SubHub\nWhile not ideal, players would default to spawning in a solo world.  This world would be uncustomizable to encourage players to go out and find a new hub.  It would contain a portal to the main hub, as well as several messages to help new players get started.  New players would also be given the option to join a “Hub Exchange Program,” which would assign them to a random hub with other users that also opted into this program. \n\nHub Exchange Program\n\tThis program would ideally work to aquant new people.  After filling out a request, a player would then be assigned a subhub with a select group of others that also opted into the program. These players would share a persistent hub where they could meet when first spawning in.  Players would have the option to change to a different hub, however there should be a time constraint to this to discourage hub hopping.\n\tCertain restrictions could be applied to applicants to this program, such as a prerequisite amount of playtime.  Situations may arise where after players stop returning, a hub becomes barren.  It should be up to the players to decide to change hubs in this case.  These hubs should feel stable and consistent to improve the sense of community in these smaller groups. \n\n\nBulletin Board \nThese hubs could have a bulletin board, a place where users can leave messages for each other in a shared virtual space.  These hubs would likely end up feeling empty, and a bulletin board would allow members of a hub to leave messages for each other, and help them to arrange meetup times for those that have no other way to contact each other.\n\nPersonalized Spawn Locations \n\tAnother idea that fell neatly from this.  In these hubs, players could have a personal spawn location where they would spawn each time.  They could each have a “gate” with their name over it so that players would know the other members of the hub even when they were not there.  This could act as a “local address” in the virtual world.\n \nChanging Settings\n\tIdeally, Players would have the ability to change/set hubs ingame.While there are many ways this can be accomplished, one idea that I had was that there could be a menu in the main hub where players would go to change hub settings.  To create a hub, players might gather all prospective members of this new hub in one instance of the main hub and join through this menu.  Similarly, users might meetup and join an existing hub though this method, although only one current member would be required.\nTo change the hub world itself, users could choose from a number of pre-existing default worlds, or from the worlds that they have uploaded.  This would also be set through this menu.  Of course this also leads to a number of questions on who would have the rights to change the world settings.  For this, you could probably defer to how other MMO’s have handled guild systems.  \nWhy not use the already existing menu?  Quite simply, changing hub settings should have some weight to it.  Players might accidentally mess up their settings if it is too easily accessible.  Similarly, you would not want to handle joining a hub through the pre existing invite system, as these can easily get confused. The ideal solution would then be an in-world menu or, less ideally, a web based solution.\n\nExample World\nID:  wrld_289006e2-21bd-4e36-9851-0900ac9f72ab",
